## 7. Behavior changes / plan deviations, with reasons

- **Real bug found and fixed: `addReaction`'s daily-cap bump never persisted in db mode.** The
  original statement (as built by the store subagent) had TWO sibling data-modifying CTEs writing
  `agent_rate_limits` for the same row within one statement — `rate_locked` (an `UPDATE` that rolls
  the day) and `bump` (a second `UPDATE` that adds 1, gated on `EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM inserted)`).
  I reproduced this independently against the real Neon dev database (outside the test suite, with a
  standalone probe) and confirmed: once a THIRD CTE (`under_cap`) reads `rate_locked`'s `RETURNING`,
  `bump`'s write is silently lost — the final `SELECT` and `RETURNING` both report success, but the
  row on disk never changes. A minimal 3-CTE reproduction (`rate_locked` → `under_cap` reading it →
  `bump`) fails the same way with no INSERT involved at all, and neither a `FROM`-join rewrite of
  `bump` nor removing the unrelated CTEs individually fixed it — only removing the SECOND writer
  entirely did. **Fix:** `src/lib/store/reactions/db.ts`'s `addReactionStatementText` now has exactly
  ONE writer of `agent_rate_limits`: `pre` (a `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`, no write) computes and locks
  the rolled count, and a single `rate_updated` `UPDATE` rolls the day AND conditionally adds 1 in one
  expression, gated on `EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM inserted)`. Verified against the real database:
  fresh react, duplicate, second distinct emoji, and over-cap all classify and persist correctly; the
  full integration suite (8/8) and the concurrent-duplicate-reacts race both pass. I did not chase a
  root cause deeper into Postgres/Neon internals beyond confirming the fix generalizes — the
  documented takeaway for future work in this codebase: **do not give two sibling CTEs a write to the
  same table/row in one statement, even when a third CTE only reads one of them** — this is a
  narrower, stricter version of the "PROJECTS its classification" rule this milestone already
  documents, and CLAUDE.md's existing invariants list doesn't yet call this specific shape out.
- **`addReaction`'s classification refined: `already_reacted` now wins over `rate_limited`
  when both are true.** Independent of the bug above, the first-draft statement computed `over_cap`
  from the post-lock count alone, so an agent who had ALREADY reacted to something and was
  SEPARATELY over their daily cap was misreported `rate_limited` instead of `already_reacted` — since
  the insert's own `WHERE ... FROM subject, under_cap` requires `under_cap` to have a row before
  `ON CONFLICT` ever gets a chance to fire, over-cap masked the real duplicate. Added a small
  `existing` CTE (read BEFORE the insert, independent of the cap) so the classification always
  reports a genuine duplicate as `already_reacted`, cap status or not — a duplicate write costs
  nothing and reports nothing new regardless of which refusal label attaches to it, but the label
  itself should name what is actually true. This is a KISS-scope refinement I made directly rather
  than looping back through a subagent, since it was a single self-contained CTE addition.
  Documented in `src/lib/store/reactions/db.ts`'s own comments.
- **Fixed two pre-existing tests broken by the new `reactions` serializer field**
  (`src/__tests__/api/v1/feed-cold-start.test.ts`, `src/__tests__/api/v1/envelope-contract.test.ts`,
  `src/__tests__/api/v1/ux7-contracts.test.ts`, `src/__tests__/api/v1/m11-2-u3-posts-characterization.test.ts`)
  — each either mocked `@/lib/store` without a `getReactionCounts` entry (three files, added
  `getReactionCounts: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({})`/`async () => ({})`) or asserted an exact
  response shape via `toEqual` that the new `reactions: {}` field now legitimately changes (one
  file, updated the expected object and the sorted key list). These are consequences of this lane's
  own deliverable 9, so fixing them is this lane's responsibility, not a cross-lane note.
- **`src/lib/agent-runtime/index.ts` edited even though the lane spec never names it.** Without this,
  `src/__tests__/lib/agent-tools/registry.test.ts` fails for this lane's two new tools specifically
  (confirmed: after the fix, only Lane D's DM tools and the shared tool-count ceiling remain failing
  in that file). Treated as an unavoidable consequence of adding a new terminal tool, same class of
  fix as the notifications-consumer wiring.
- **No other deviations.** The store shape, the action, the routes, the tools and the notification
  all match the spec's deliverable list; `getReactionCounts` batching avoids N+1 everywhere it was
  wired.
116,893
1. **MAJOR — Reaction notifications survive post deletion.** [notifications/db.ts:519](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/notifications/db.ts:519), [notifications/memory.ts:483](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/notifications/memory.ts:483). Violates P6.2, line 351. Both notification types omit `metadata.post_id`, but `deletePost` uses that field for cleanup. React, drain the event, then delete the post. The notification retains the deleted content title and link. **Minimal fix:** Add the parent `post_id` to both notification types in both stores. Test notification removal after a drain.

2. **MAJOR — Comment locks can reverse the required order.** [reactions/db.ts:48](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/reactions/db.ts:48), [notifications/db.ts:509](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/notifications/db.ts:509). Violates the `posts → comments → agents` invariant. Both joins lock comments and posts without an explicit dependency that locks the post first. A reaction can hold the comment while `deletePost` holds the post. Each then waits for the other row, which causes a deadlock. **Minimal fix:** Make the comment lock depend on a prior CTE that locks the live post.

3. **MAJOR — Memory mode accepts a withdrawn actor.** [reactions/memory.ts:43](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/reactions/memory.ts:43). Violates the actor re-check invariant. An actor can withdraw during an action’s subject or group read. The memory store then adds the reaction, charges quota, and emits an event. PostgreSQL refuses the reaction through its actor foreign key. **Minimal fix:** Check the actor by ID before any memory mutation. Add a test that withdraws the actor during the action’s read.

4. **MAJOR — The deletion tests do not exercise the required races.** [m11-2-b1-reactions.test.ts:149](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/__tests__/integration/m11-2-b1-reactions.test.ts:149). Violates P6.2, line 351. Both deletion orders use sequential calls. The delete-first case can return from the action before the store statement runs. Removal of the subject locks would therefore leave these tests green. The reaction-first case also omits notification checks. **Minimal fix:** Use controlled, overlapping transactions for both orders and subject types. Drain before deletion and verify notification removal. The scoped tests also need nonempty serializer checks, exact retry-time checks, and concurrent duplicates in memory mode.

5. **MINOR — A concurrent duplicate can report `rate_limited`.** [reactions/db.ts:70](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/reactions/db.ts:70). Violates the recorded duplicate-precedence rule. Two identical requests start with one quota slot left. After the first commits, the second locked quota read sees the full count. However, its `existing` read can retain the earlier snapshot and miss the reaction. The second request then reports `rate_limited`. **Minimal fix:** Acquire the rate lock before the decisive statement takes its snapshot, within the transaction. Test concurrent duplicates at the cap.

6. **MINOR — The seed and reaction write use separate transactions.** [reactions/db.ts:108](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/reactions/db.ts:108). Violates the two-statement batch requirement in P6.2, line 350. The seed commits before event validation and the decisive statement. An invalid event or statement failure therefore leaves the seed row behind. **Minimal fix:** Validate the events first, then execute both statements in one `sql.transaction` batch.

7. **MINOR — Removal omits the required subject lock.** [reactions/db.ts:155](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/reactions/db.ts:155), [reactions/memory.ts:94](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/reactions/memory.ts:94). Violates P6.2, line 350. A store call against a stale reaction on deleted content still removes the row and emits `reaction.removed`. Neither store checks subject liveness. **Minimal fix:** Gate removal on the locked live subject in PostgreSQL. Apply the equivalent memory check. Keep removal uncapped.

8. **MINOR — A JSON `null` body causes an exception.** [posts reaction route:58](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/app/api/v1/posts/[id]/reactions/route.ts:58), [comments reaction route:58](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/app/api/v1/comments/[id]/reactions/route.ts:58). Violates the lane’s request-validation contract. Both methods accept JSON `null`, then access `body.emoji` outside the error handler. The request fails instead of returning 400. **Minimal fix:** Validate the parsed body before field access. Test POST and DELETE on both routes.

9. **NIT — The SQL comment gives an incorrect restriction on the PostgreSQL rule.** [reactions/db.ts:52](/Users/mohsin/Github/safemolt/src/lib/store/reactions/db.ts:52). This conflicts with the lane’s request for simple code and comments. The single-writer fix is correct. PostgreSQL does not support two updates to one row in one statement, regardless of a third reader. The comment can mislead a later maintainer into restoring two writers without that reader. **Minimal fix:** Replace the paragraph with the general rule and its [PostgreSQL reference](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-with.html#QUERIES-WITH-MODIFYING).

